On Wed, 27 Dec 2006, tohris wrote:

> I was just wondering why, as soon as we got all the bricks (instruction
> set, portions of the original EV7 arch...), would it be impossible to
> get the Alpha back to life again! It would be hard, but the arch was so
> great, that with today's tech (45nm, SOC/SOI, tridimensional
> connections), it could certainly outperform a brand new Intel Core 2
> chip...

That would be hard to do!

The Core 2 is a damn good chip -- just using "the Alpha architecture" 
would not be enough to beat it.  It takes lots of clever implementation 
tricks and lots of elbow grease to just match it in performance.

First you would have to spend an exorbitant amount of money, then you 
would need have to spend time in order to have your people learn about the 
microarchitecture and what circuit tricks are relevant, considering the 
precise process it is going to be built in.

And if you succeed, then what?

What software would it run?  Who would buy it?

The Alpha was elegant in many ways but the best CPUs these days are 
x86-compatibles from Intel and AMD (+ probably the coming Power6).

-Peter


