>
>All you'd need is money.  A great big pile of money.  Would this be a
>hobby project, or are you interested in some sort of commercial success?
>
>For starters, you'd need to pay HP enough to persuade them to let you use
>the intellectual property.  I have no idea how much that would take. 
>You'd certainly have to pay them enough to cover the cost of finding all
>the IP, and the lawyers to search for hidden risks in the contract.
>
>There's enough (or almost enough) of the Alpha architecture already
>published to  let you build an implementation.  Selling that
>implementation would require HP's permission.
>
>How would you implement Alpha?  You could simulate the whole thing in
>software.  You could implement it in cheap, programmable hardware.  Either
>of those would probably give terrible performance.
>
>If you want to surpass the performance of EV7z, all you have to do is
>implement the Alpha architecture in some modern technology.  And get rid
>of enough of the bugs to make it "work".  That last sentence is the hard
>part, and the one that would likely use up the most money.
>
>Making EV7 work was _hard_.  The financial return barely justified the
>investment.  I suspect making any advanced CPU work right is very hard,
>and in most cases the cost of doing so can't be justified.
>
>It seemed to me that the EV7 effort had at least 4 major parts:
>1. Choice of CPU features to be implemented, and design of actual
>implementation.
>2. Development of the fabrication process.
>3. Simulations of signals and connections in the proposed chip design.
>4. Testing and debugging of samples of the CPU.

<AHEM!>  You have left out the most important support step: software
development that uses the architecture.  Without that, your hardware
is a boat anchor...for a very small boat.
>
>All of these happened in parallel, and they all fed back into the others
>through multiple iterations.  Eventually the result was close enough to
>correct to mass-produce and ship.
>
>If you change one part, like the fabrication process, you get to pretty
>much start over on all 4 fronts.  The idea of an easy "process shrink"
>applying to a complex chip design is pretty much a fantasy.
>
>If you took the final EV7z design and "shrunk" it to "45nm, SOC/SOI,
>tridimensional connections" (to quote buzzwords without fully
>understanding them), I would expect the first sample to do absoulutely
>NOTHING.  The clocks probably wouldn't start and syncronize, and the CPU
>woulnd't fetch or exectute a single Alpha instruction successfully.  If
>you were lucky, you'd get some useful debug traces out of it before it
>burned itself up.
>
>Of course, experience creates luck.  Unless you re-assembled a bunch of
>EV7 expertise, your luck would be mostly bad for several iterations.

Relearning others' mistakes is a problem that I haven't figured
how to eliminate.  That's one of the primary problems in the
current computing biz...the group of people who know what not
to do (because they've already done it) get dispersed with every
company sale, new project, or passage of time.

/BAH


