On Thu, 28 Dec 2006 11:09:45 +0100, "Peter \"Firefly\" Lund"
<firefly@diku.dk> wrote:

>Got any more information on the HyperVAX?  I'd really like to see how its 
>microarchitecture worked.
>

HyperVAX was one of two AD studies done by Dave Orbits and Dave Cane,
circa 1982 - 1983.  The other one was called SuperVAX, or maybe
UltraVAX.  I think the former.

I wasn't on either team.  I was working on Venus at the time.

The questions they tried to answer was, what would it take technically
to build an extremely fast VAX, as measured by TPI?  Ancillary
questions were, could the architectural bottlenecks be identified and
remedies proposed?

The details are fuzzy now, but I think one study assumed a particular
technology family, while the other assumed that an infinite amount of
hardware could be thrown at the problem.  (This was intended to see
what the upper bound on "VAX performance" (again, in TPI) was.)

One of the observations was the architecture had so many intricacies
that throwing infinite hardware didn't help much.  With each iteration
of hypothetical hardware to remove one bottleneck, they would speed up
a little bit just to hit another bottleneck.  In hindsight that
doesn't sound transcendental, but at the time it was an ah-ha moment.
Maybe I'm not remembering it right.

I used to have a copy of one of the final reports.  It's either buried
in the garage or long gone.  Along with my set of "Why Jupiter?"
memos.  Alas...


