On Thu, 28 Dec 2006 17:46:12 +0100, "Peter \"Firefly\" Lund"
<firefly@diku.dk> wrote:

>On Thu, 28 Dec 2006, John DeRosa wrote:
>
>> I wasn't on either team.  I was working on Venus at the time.
>
>8600, ECL gate arrays, 80ns cycle time?

Yes.  A whopping 5W per gate array, which at the time was considered
to be incredible.

>
>Didn't you waste a lot of time by throwing away the VIC and the TLB too 
>often?

I don't recognize the acronym, "VIC".  Virtual Instruction Counter? We
didn't use that term.... I forget what we called the various Ibox
PC's, but I don't think VIC was one of them.  The prefetch buffer we
just called the, "prefetch buffer".  Or IBUF.

Every design is a trade-off.  And in many things the logic family
you're using dictates, or strongly suggests, many of the decisions.  A
great example is cache size -- the density and organization of your
memory chips will dictate where the sweet spot is in terms of cache
size and structure at a given cycle time.  Go beyond that point, and
building a larger cache becomes very hard.

We were studying and reading about VAX performance all the time, of
course.  We were beginning to believe that 512 bytes was too small a
page size.  But nobody felt that that, or any other architectural
issues, was a critical, "you-bet-your-business" issue.

John


