>mostly still unsolved.  I was never inside DEC when it was DEC.  I was a
>customer.  A frustrated, annoyed customer.  I saw many problems with DEC,
>from the outside.

There were always problems.  That's life.  The key was that those
who could have worked on improvement didn't know what the problems 
were.  DEC got too big too fast.  To keep costs down, only managers
were sent to DECUS.  The new young workers, both hardware and software,
never met customers and listened to their bitches.  The biz became
"professional" and the human element at the lower echelon of _both_
customer and Digital was removed.


>Now I see a lot of problems that cause and contribute to the customers'
>frustrations.  Two companies and a decade or two later, and the old timers
>indicate that these problems aren't new.

Of course not.  It's called human nature and problems that are
a result of this cannot be fixed without the people involved,
not their higher level managers, talking^Warguing with each
other.
>
>>> then you'd be well on your way to rescuing dozens of
>>>big companies that are way to the dustbin.
>>
>>Nope.  It doesn't work.
>>
>>>
>>>Unfortunately, that appears to be very difficult.  Perhaps impossibly
>>>difficult. Every big organization I've seen from the inside is so messed
>>>up, the mind boggles when trying to decide what to fix first.  Everybody
>>>has a different opinion about what is important.
>>
>>Your customer is the most important.
>
>That's widely understood, both inside and outside. 

No.  It is not understood.  Oh, people pay verbal homage to this
tenet but not many understand it.

> Yet the big, obvious
>problems that dissatisfy customers remain unsolved.  HP had evolved its
>own version of most of them, with no help at all from DEC.

All companies do.  Each one has its own corporate folklore.  That
folklore will cause certain problems.  To fix those by eliminating
would not work.  It's an interesting problem.
>
>I do not believe there is an understanding of how to make the organization
>get better.  It is much harder to fix a big organization than a big chuck
>of software or hardware.

Some days, that is true :-).
>
>>>Or am I too cynical?
>>
>>No, just ignorant about how things work in commerce and trade.
>
>Yes, I'm ignorant about how things "work".  I've rarely seen things
>"work".  But I'm very familiar with how they DON'T work.

No, you only see the results when something doesn't seem to work.
We, in coding world, would call that the symptom.  Finding the 
"how" is part of the diagnosis piece of fixing a problem.  An
obvious cure may not be the best fix if it creates more unwanted
side effects than the orginal problem.  When this happened in
our software biz, we would categorize the problem as an asspect
and document it as a constraint (a fact of the computer systems'
life).  Another description of this kind of decision making can
be called trade-off depending on when, during the development
and/or maintenance process, the decision was made.

/BAH



