>jmfbahciv@aol.com wrote:
>
>....
>
>><AHEM!>  You have left out the most important support step: software
>>development that uses the architecture.  Without that, your hardware
>>is a boat anchor...for a very small boat.
>
>Of course.  But he didn't say he wanted software, or commercial success. 
>Just a fancy new Alpha CPU.

Oh, yea.  You have a point.  I just can't think of making something
that can't be used :-).  It's not in my DNA.
>
>I think there's a widespread misconception that great CPUs automatically
>make great systems, or great customer solutions.  Silly idea, but the
>trade press seems mostly  unwilling or unable to focus on the bigger
>picture.

Yes.  I've talked about this at times.  My usual metric is something
I've always called user-thruput, or how much work any ordinary user
of the hard/software can get done without having to repeat his/her
keystrokes over a period of wallclock time.
>
>Even the people who talk to customers about my products like to lead with
>Intel's latest CPU improvements.  Not HP's system or software improvements
>that outnumber those in the CPU by probably a factor of 10 or more.

Well, this was always a "guy thing".  People understand faster gear
and extrapolate this to "better".  Some days it is better, some
days it ain't.
>
>....
>
>>Relearning others' mistakes is a problem that I haven't figured
>>how to eliminate.  That's one of the primary problems in the
>>current computing biz...the group of people who know what not
>>to do (because they've already done it) get dispersed with every
>>company sale, new project, or passage of time.
>
>Very true, and very frustrating.

I've been looking at how the science biz has worked this out.
The problem is that engineering doesn't quite have the same
"rules" as research since the goal is to make a profit.  The
minute one figures out what is wrong, the wrong is eliminated
and replace with the corrected way.  No time nor money can
be spent on writing up which small things didn't work.  Only
the really, really big bloops is worthwhile documenting.

>
>There's also the opposite problem of people who never learn, and keep
>making the same mistakes in every company or project.  Weeding them out
>(or at least most of them) seems to be a very rare skill.

In an ideal world there wouldn't be a need to spend effort on weeding
them out.  If you keep your IP group together, those who don't do
much will evolve out of the group.  The computing biz has just
gone through an era (I hope it's over) where these core groups
were broken up and reassigned.  I don't know how it will work out.
I'm not sure if the automobile industry ever went through this
growing pain.  hmm...thanks, you've just given me something
to think about.
>
>It was interesting to see that the EV7 team avoided most of the biggest
>problems that plagued the EV6 products.  Not by luck; they planned for
>problems and built debugging tools that saved probably years of
>time-to-market. 

Yes.  That's because they knew the nature of the tools they would need.
This can only be done if there is a colletive man-centuries of
experience at all levels working on the project from Day 1.

The problems with having so many cooks is obvious.  That's where
having management becomes important.

> Of course new problems appeared, as they always do when
>the design pushes the envelope.

Of course.  Every fix had side effects.  If you fixed a problem,
the methods the users used to avoid the problem, no longer 
"works" and will likely be viewed as a bug because the perception
of the behaviour changed.  I have extraordinary user habits to
avoid the problems I've met using Windows.  All of them
are now a habit which I no longer have to think about--all the
coping is known only by fingers.  If stuff gets fixed or changed,
I have to spend thinking and wallclock time adjusted to the
new behaviour.  

There has to be similar cases with hardware.

Now, think about the cases where _code_ did the coping for
unwanted system behaviour.  Now you have the coping in bits.
The new systems have to consider these coping methods has
a requirement even if the circumstances are really considered
to be a bug.  We used to call this backwards compatible.

It's a corrolary in the computing biz that the correct fix
is rarely the "right" fix.

/BAH


/BAH


