I found a few stories people had written about them.  A recurrent theme 
was that they didn't work well and required lots and lots of field service 
and replaced circuit boards.  I think one of the stories was about a place 
where they celebrated the first time their VAX 9000 stayed up for more 
than 30 days.  This was about a year and a half after they'd got it.

[John again]
> It was a technological tour-de-force and cul-de-sac.  They pushed the
> outside of the envelope on everything.
>  While it is unfair to say that
> the VAX 9000 caused the demise of DEC, it definitely contributed to
> its woes.

I don't think the VAX 9000 alone could have killed DEC, either, but that 
it was one of several big (and unnecessary) blows DEC took.

Gordon Bell claims it was a $1B investment, but he may have reason to 
exaggerate a bit.

-Peter


