On Sun, 24 Dec 2006 jmfbahciv@aol.com wrote:

> In article <Pine.LNX.4.61.0612231654260.30928@ask.diku.dk>,
>   "Peter \"Firefly\" Lund" <firefly@diku.dk> wrote:
>> On Fri, 22 Dec 2006 jmfbahciv@aol.com wrote:
>>
>>> Talk to an experienced programmer before you JRST to this conclusion.
>>
>> I am an experienced programmer.
>
> Yes.  From what you written, my guess is that you are a bitty
> programmer and don't need to align stuff to sort and move bits
> like us mere mortals need to do. :-)

What precisely do you mean by "bitty"?  It seems to have several, quite 
different meanings in English.

>>>  Byte addressing has similar problems.  If the
>>> bits aren't left nor right justified, the programmer thas
>>> to pad the bit-deck with noops.
>>>
>>>
>>>>  It made driver
>>>> code a lot harder to port.  It also meant that individual single-byte
>>>> variables that happened to be too close together couldn't be accessed
>>>> atomically -- even though the source code showed that everything was fine.
>>>
>>> You should work with cards physically.  You might get a glimmer of
>>> bit arrangements and ease of use.
>>
>> I am not enough of a mind reader to figure what you are talking about ;)
>
> I was muttering ideas.  I was wondering if there was a correlation
> between having to smooth physical data had anything to do with
> our (the mere mortal coders) need to have stuff word aligned.

An aligner in the processor is more complex hardware-wise than not having 
one.  And it can be hard to hide its latency.  Adding another pipeline 
stage is not attractive and only adding it "conditionally" is not 
attractive either.  I can certainly sympathesize with the Alpha designers. 
I just feel it was a dumb decision because software is important, too.

The very lose memory ordering was another brilliant decision for the 
hardware in isolation but not for the software and therefore not for the 
system as a whole.  Especially not when the previous architecture, the 
VAX, used program order.

The VAX was also cache coherent, even between its own internal caches so 
program-generated code worked fine.  The Alpha needed explicit 
synchronization.

-Peter


