>From the foreword of Digital Technical Journal's Fall 1990 issue (vol 2, 
>no 2):
>
>"In the summer of 1983, a small group of us set about to determine what it 
>would take for Digital to develop a true mainframe.  We felt that a 
>mainframe VAX would be a powerful addition to Digital's product family."
>
>(by Carl S. Gibson, VAX 9000 Program Manager)

BAH has already replied to your post, but I wanted to add a little
something.  The DTJ was a marketing enterprise.  It was designed to
showcase the greatness of DEC engineering.  (That was said without
sarcasm.  DEC produced a lot of great engineering, and the DTJ was
conceived as a means to get the word out.)

But vehicles like technical journals differ in how much marketing spin
is put on the engineering realities.  In the case of DTJ, they often
were too close to the marketing end of the spectrum.  Take everything
you read there, especially references to dates, amount of difficulty,
and "why we chose to do it this way" sorts of explanations, with a
grain of salt.

John


