>That sounds better.  Wasn't this when people started with the BS
>sessions and was not yet a project?

Well, defining the official start date of an engineering project is
sometimes a matter of who's doing the talking.  E.g., the guys working
on HyperVAX (whom I personally knew) might say that Aquarius' start
date should be pegged to them.  (Or maybe not, depending on their
opinion of the 9000 :-) )

There were engineers working on Aquarius' architecture and technology
in late 1984.  Earlier in 1984, they were all involved in the push to
get the 8600 out the door, and they transitioned to (or, back to)
Aquarius work as that effort succeeded.  In fact, "all hands on deck"
was officially declared to get Venus out the door.  So all Large VAX
Engineering projects were officially suspended, and all bodies were
officially available for Venus debugging work in one way or another.
(Although the odd individual here & there managed to continue working
on their official project.)

So for what I consider a sufficient nucleus of people to call a
project real, I'd peg the VAX 9000 as starting in late 1984.  But was
technology and architectural work happening before then?  Sure.

