On Tue, 26 Dec 2006, John DeRosa wrote:

> BAH has already replied to your post, but I wanted to add a little
> something.  The DTJ was a marketing enterprise.  It was designed to
> showcase the greatness of DEC engineering.  (That was said without
> sarcasm.  DEC produced a lot of great engineering, and the DTJ was
> conceived as a means to get the word out.)

Sure.  I use DTJ mostly for the technical stuff.  If they write that a 
certain self-timed ECL register chip designed and built for the VAX 
9000 can do a write operation in say 5ns, then I tend to believe that it 
really did.  I also tend to believe their diagrams of the 
microarchitecture, barring bugs and simplifications for explanatory 
purposes.

And when they brag about how complex the VAX 9000 was (and they did) then 
I do believe them.  Everything they described technically about the 
machine backed that up.

And everytime they thumped their chest and said "look how difficult this 
was, ain't we cool?"  I would read "look how bad our risk management is -- 
can you believe we did something this stupid?"

I'm impressed that they were able to solve so many hard problems so 
quickly but I'm not impressed that they chose to.

-Peter


