I think a better analogy would be the architect says the buyer needs
to re-license the design or he won't be allowed to move in.  The
house is like the hardware; there are no issues with that, you can
buy it and use it for anything you want (paper weight, boat anchor,
foot warmer, computer, spare parts), you just can't run any of the
originally-licensed software on it.

Barb's followup to this, claiming that it isn't like you bought the
house, it's like you rented the house, so of course the new tenant
has to pay rent, is a straw man.  You didn't rent the house, you
bought it, and you bought a perpetual license to use it.  (One-time
payment, which has already been made by the original purchaser, not
repeating payments.)

This is not to say HP or Mentec doesn't have the right to put the
restrictions they have on their property, they do (at least in so
far as their licensing terms are legal under the prevailing
jurisdiction.)

If a (housing) developer and an architect agreed to put terms in
a deed or purchase and sale agreement, or what ever, that said the
buyer couldn't resell the house without paying a fee to the builder
and/or architect (to protect their IP), who would buy a house with
such a restriction?  (Probably lots of people, but their lawyer
should advise them not to, or demand a significant reduction in
price or something, because they are really going to regret it
later!)  Another problem with this analogy is that houses generally
appreciate in value, unless seriously abused or there is some kind
of disaster (Katrina).  But the hardware we are discussing here is
virtually worthless - the original owner is throwing it away or
selling it at pennies on the dollar.

People assume that if the hardware is "free for the taking, save
it from the dumpster", the software is too.  It apparently isn't
because it seems Mentec is still selling PDP-11 software licenses
(and getting traditional prices for them.)  Due to licensing
restrictions, there is no market in used PDP-11 licenses, but
this doesn't mean new PDP-11 licenses are worthless, the used
ones are worthless only because they can't legally be used except by
the original purchaser.  (What happens if the original purchaser
is bought or merges with another company, or if it spins off the
division that owned the particular computer, and that division/
new company wants to continue running the computer and its software?
Do they have to do a license transfer?)

I personally feel it would be nice if Mentec set up a hobbyist
program to allow non-commercial use of all their software.  I don't
really think it would be all that hard to do.  They would basically
need to set up a web page with a boiler-plate license, and an "I
agree" button that would send then an email saying John Doe at
some address had accepted the terms of the license.  This kind
of license should be fairly cheap to set up; HP/Montagar did it,
and so do Process Software (for TCPware, Multinet and PDMF), and
so does Raxco (PerfectDisk), and a bunch of other 3rd-party VMS
vendors.  Not to mention zillions of PC and Mac software vendors,
who offer free (often limited in functionality) versions of their
software, things like Eudora, Free Agent (news reader), screen
savers, etc. etc.

They wouldn't need to do anything with the email they get except
file it.

Someone who was trying to cheat Mentec of commercial revenue would
*not* sign up for this license, it would only make it easier to
track them down.  But when suing cheaters, Mentec could submit
its license terms to prove it was protecting its property.  (I
don't know if it matters, some people in this thread have claimed
IP never becomes public domain, no matter what, which is nonsense,
both patents and copyrights eventually expire, and others have
claimed that if the property isn't protected, it can lapse into
the public domain.  I don't know which claim is correct, and it
might depend on what country or state has jurisdiction.)

Contrary to what others have claimed, I don't think setting up
or administering such a program would be enormously expensive.
If you already have a web site, putting up a licensing page would
only take a competent web designer an hour or two (if that), and
I think you can get boiler-plate for a suitable shrink-wrap
license agreement from anywhere, and a competent lawyer should
be able to customize one as needed very quickly.  I even think
it might be possible to get a bunch of us to chip in to fund the
legal fees for this (as a one-time thing.)  Or maybe we could
get Encompass to spend some of the $90 I send them every year
on this project.  (After all, Encompass was originally DECUS,
which dealt with PDP-11's before VAXes were a glint in Gordon
Bell's eye.)

If Mentec were to charge for the hobbyist license, I think it
would cost them a lot more to administer.  They'd have to keep
track, set up a payment system with VISA, Mastercard, PayPal,
etc., or deal with checks and money orders (in foreign funds,
the PDP-11 community is global), handle the accounting and
funds transfers, etc.  But even then, there are zillions of
small, web based mom-and-pop businesses that have no problem
doing this, so I don't think it is all that difficult.


