> 
> Someone who was trying to cheat Mentec of commercial revenue would
> *not* sign up for this license, it would only make it easier to
> track them down.  But when suing cheaters, Mentec could submit
> its license terms to prove it was protecting its property.  (I
> don't know if it matters, some people in this thread have claimed
> IP never becomes public domain, no matter what, which is nonsense,
> both patents and copyrights eventually expire, and others have

This is true, but under current law copyrights won't expire until
long after all of us are little more than dust.  Did you know that
the characters "Sherlock Holmes and Dr. John Watson" are still
copyrighted.  And the copyright is held by an estate as there is
apparently no current heir. 

> claimed that if the property isn't protected, it can lapse into
> the public domain.  I don't know which claim is correct, and it
> might depend on what country or state has jurisdiction.)

This is true of Trade Marks and Service Marks, I do not believe it
is true of copyrights.  I believe they remain in effect until they
expire.  If not renewed the item falls into the public domain.  But,
as I said above, and you can easily verify on the net, under current
law both in the US and the EU (I believe international copyright is
based on an aghreement signed in Basel, Switzerland) current copyrights
will outlast all of us by a rather long time.

> 
> Contrary to what others have claimed, I don't think setting up
> or administering such a program would be enormously expensive.

If it cost $1, what would be Mentec's incentive?  What would they
get out of it?  The admiration of a group of people who have been
insulting them and  stealing their IP for years?  

> If you already have a web site, putting up a licensing page would
