On Sat, 18 Nov 2006, Patrick MOREAU, DTI Athis ex CENA, Tel: 01.69.57.68.40 wrote:

> Alpha is not really a VAX extension but a new RISC architecture which emulates
> some of VAX architecture functions (ex atomicity of certain operations) using
> PAL code.

And the VAX floating-point formats.  Normal IEEE floating-point, i.e. when 
there are no INFs, or NaNs, is almost identical to VAX floating-point. 
The biggest difference is that the exponent has a slightly different bias 
(2 more or less than IEEE).  VAX fp doesn't have all the exceptional stuff 
that's in IEEE: +/- INF, NaN, denorms, various rounding modes, various 
trapping modes, ...

This means that it was probably relatively cheap to make a floating-point 
unit that could handle both IEEE and VAX formats, whereas it would have 
been very expensive to emulate the VAX formats in software, even given 
IEEE hardware.

> The VAX architecture was very complicated especially if you see
> addressing modes (but at the time of very small memory it was probably a good
> thing). The VAX IS was so complicated that some instructions were not available
> on the Cmos implementations (decimal instructions IIRW) but emulated in
> software.

I'm not sure I would say that the addressing modes were the most 
complicated part of the VAX architecture.  The EDITPC instruction probably 
was.

-Peter


