Camiel wrote:

> I'm trying to figure out the layout of data in the NVRAM / last 50
> bytes of TOY memory on a DS20. Could anyone help me by using the
> "save_nvram srm" and "save_nvram toy"  commands, and mailing the
> resulting files to me? This data from a DS20 would be most useful to
> me, but data from another system would be welcome to, as I don't expect
> this to have changed a lot.


   Within OpenVMS (and depending on what you are up to here), there are 
various symbolic offsets for the nvram in the system definitions 
library.  Look for the substring TOY or NVR within the symbols.  These 
for the pieces that OpenVMS (overtly) uses.  (And if you don't have an 
OpenVMS box handy, these libraries are accessible via the eisner and 
deathrow systems.)

   The nvram has the bb_watch (toy), environment variables, and system 
configuration information, and the console code.  The nvram is as much 
as 8KB IIRC, and all sorts of stuff can be in found there up to and 
including a console of your choosing, so I'm not sure what you mean by 
"TOY memory" nor what you might be looking to do.

   There are some manuals around for the AlphaPC 164 series SBC IIRC, 
that have some details.  Google for "164 srm nvram site:digital.com" as 
a start.

   Followups have been set to c.o.v.



